import SiteShell from '../layouts/SiteShell.astro';
---

<SiteShell description="Ollie's corner of the web">
  <h2>Welcome to my website</h2>
  <p>
    You may be thinking to yourself, "Oliver, your website looks a little crazy." I know, right? It's an homage to the
    early internet and GeoCities era of personal site dev and it has been a ton of fun to work on. If you don't know me,
    you can probably guess my name :^)
  </p>
  <p>
    So why a website? In the late nineties many software devs and enthusiasts began creating their own websites to share
    their interests and connect with others. My dad created{' '}
    <a href="https://web.archive.org/web/20020609221118/http://www.theohrts.com/" target="_blank" rel="noopener">
      theohrts.com
    </a>{' '}
    in the year 2000 (if you've ever sent me an email, now you know where my domain comes from). While the site is no
    longer active and only partially viewable through the Wayback Machine, it's a cool little piece of family history.
    Before the homogenization of websites, thanks to AI, many folks had their own quirky part of the internet to call
    their own.
  </p>
  <p>
    This site aims to partake in what was once lost. But this is not unique to me. There's a whole community of
    developers building their own sites by hand again à la the{' '}
    <a href="https://indieweb.org/" target="_blank" rel="noopener">
      IndieWeb
    </a>
    , a movement that champions owning your domain, words, and data instead of renting a profile from a platform. I've
    been iterating on this project since high school and I'm happy with where it's at. I think it's a good reflection of
    my interests and personality, and what it lacks in professionalism it gains back in character.
  </p>
  <p>
    Check out <a href="/webmaster/">Webmaster</a> to learn more about me and the tech behind the site, and{' '}
    <a href="/links/">Links</a> for where else to find me and the sites / people I like. Off site, my{' '}
    <a href="https://blog.ohrt.dev" target="_blank" rel="noopener">
      weblog
    </a>{' '}
    has my technical writings and my projects page has the things I've built.
  </p>
  <p>
    Thanks for stopping by. <a href="/guestbook/">Sign the guestbook</a> on your way out.
  </p>
</SiteShell>
